.SH THEMES
With \fBtheme = auto\fR (the default), kmd uses \fBlight\-theme\fR on a
light terminal background and \fBdark\-theme\fR otherwise. The background is
taken from \fBCOLORFGBG\fR when it names a color; otherwise kmd asks the terminal
(OSC 11, followed by a device attributes request that nearly every terminal
answers), but only when colors are on and both standard input and output
are the terminal: never into a pipe, with \fB\-\-html\fR or with
\fBTERM=dumb\fR. kmd gives up on a terminal that stays silent after 0.1 s
(1 s over SSH) and then uses the dark theme. \fBkmd \-\-config\fR shows which
source decided. Some terminals take several milliseconds to answer (about
16 ms for VTE-based ones such as GNOME Terminal); setting a theme skips the
detection and that wait.
.PP
A theme file \fI~/.config/kmd/themes/NAME.theme\fR contains \fBkey = #rrggbb\fR
lines. \fBinherit\fR selects the base theme. The palette keys \fBbg\fR,
\fBfg\fR, \fBbright\fR, \fBmuted\fR, \fBcomment\fR, \fBborder\fR,
\fBsurface\fR, \fBon-accent\fR, \fBred\fR, \fBorange\fR, \fByellow\fR,
\fBgreen\fR, \fBcyan\fR, \fBblue\fR, \fBpurple\fR, \fBteal\fR and
\fBmagenta\fR re-derive every color; individual roles (for example
\fBheading2\fR, \fBcode-bg\fR or \fBsyntax-keyword\fR) can be overridden too.
Run \fBkmd \-\-theme\-template tokyo-night\fR for a complete example.
Only theme files whose names consist of ASCII letters, digits, \fB.\fR,
\fB\-\fR and \fB_\fR are listed and completed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KMD_THEME
Default theme.
.TP
.B KMD_PAGER
How long documents are shown: \fBbuiltin\fR (the default, see
\fBVIEWER\fR), a pager command such as \fBless \-R\fR, or \fB\-\fR to
disable paging.
.TP
.B KMD_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file; it must be absolute.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Folder of the configuration file and themes (\fI~/.config\fR by default);
ignored unless absolute.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colors when set (see https://no-color.org).
.TP
.B COLORFGBG
Used by the \fBauto\fR theme to detect light terminals (see \fBTHEMES\fR).
.TP
.B COLORTERM
\fBtruecolor\fR or \fB24bit\fR enables 24-bit colors.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where \fB\-\-slides\fR and \fB\-\-notes\fR keep their state file (in
\fIkmd\fR below it).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if a file could not be read or written, 2 on usage errors.
.SH REMOTE DOCUMENTS
.B kmd
never makes network requests: it has no URL arguments, does not download
images, and never contacts a server while rendering. Fetching a document
would let its author learn when and from where it is read (tracking pixels),
and would put a network client, with its TLS, proxies and redirects, inside
a tool that only needs to read text. To read a remote document, download it
with a tool made for that and pipe it in:
.PP
.RS
.nf
curl \-fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OWNER/REPO/main/README.md | kmd
.fi
.RE
.PP
A document read from standard input has no folder: its relative links and
images resolve against the current folder, as if it had been saved there.
Give \fB\-\-base\fR the document's URL to turn its relative links into
web links instead. Its images are then on the web too, so only inline
\fBdata:\fR images are drawn; the others show their alt text, since
drawing them would mean downloading them.
.SH SECURITY
Documents are treated as untrusted. Control characters, escape sequences and
bidi overrides contained in documents are removed before output, and so are
invisible format characters such as zero-width spaces (also from copied
code), so a document cannot control or visually reorder the terminal. kmd makes no
network requests and remote images are never downloaded (see
\fBREMOTE DOCUMENTS\fR). Linked documents are only read when the user follows the
link in the viewer, and only local images, PDFs and other data files (never
programs) are handed to the desktop. Clickable OSC 8 targets are only
\fBhttp\fR(\fBs\fR) URLs without a user name, \fBmailto:\fR with only a
subject and body, and \fBfile://\fR URLs of local Markdown and document
files. The viewer shows the scheme and whole host of a web link before
opening it, and refuses web links with a user name or without a plainly
written host. Reloads and linked documents are opened without blocking and
must be regular files. The only query sent to the terminal
asks for its background color (see \fBTHEMES\fR); its answer is read for
about a second at most and 256 bytes and parsed strictly. The only file
kmd writes is the slide number for \fB\-\-notes\fR, in a directory that
must belong to the user with mode 0700; \fB\-\-notes\fR reads at most 32
bytes from it, and otherwise reads the deck as \fB\-\-slides\fR does. Inputs are limited to 16 MiB, 250,000 blocks and 500,000 inline
elements (table cells included, at most 262,144 per document), images to
20 MiB and 40 megapixels (at most 50 and 64 MiB read per document, each file
read once; network and device paths are refused), and the number of
diagrams and display formulas per document is bounded, as are the bytes
reference links may copy from their definitions (1 MiB plus the document's
size). A top-level block is laid out in at most about a million styled
pieces (64 MiB) and a document renders at most 64 MiB of text; a notice
replaces the rest. Tables shown as records cut their labels to 32 columns and 128 bytes.
The
.B \-\-html
export drops control characters, bidi overrides, invisible format characters
and carriage returns too.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B kmd README.md
Preview a file.
.TP
.B kmd \-f notes.md
Live preview while editing.
.TP
.B kmd \-\-toc \-\-color=never CHANGELOG.md
Plain outline of a document.
.TP
.B kmd \-\-html doc.md > doc.html
Export to HTML.
.TP
.B kmd \-\-slides \-\-html talk.md > talk.html
Export a presentation; print it to PDF from the browser.
.TP
.B curl \-fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OWNER/REPO/main/README.md | kmd \-\-base https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/blob/main/README.md
Read a remote README, with its relative links pointing to the repository on
GitHub.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR less (1)
